Nop, casting spells is not a choice if you
- Don't have starting food
- Spend loads of turns looting shops because you haven't found any
tripe
- Let your pet eat corpses so it doesn't go feral (which, agreed,
isn't much of a problem in the Mines... but anyway, I usually go down
till lvl 6, so I max out the number of shops I find without taking
excessive risk)

Specifically, the worst problem for the healer is starvation. The
protection can be done fast (if you have tripe/apples) or slow. You
can't count on finding tripe with the healer, so it'll be done slow
more often than not. It's not unusual to visit the priest on turn
8000, or even 11000. You can't count on finding 10 food rations/
successful prayers.

Let's review the specific advantages of the Gnomish Healer:

1) Healing spell: Moot point, you shouldn't be casting spells.
2) Sthetoscope: I'll give you that when it comes to discover hidden
corridors. You waste many turns searching for them, and with a
sthetoscope it's very fast. But it won't help you much with your pet,
because it'll attack when it decides to (floating eyes, for example,
losing your sidekick for too many turns). And as I said, casting
healing isn't the best option.
3) Infravision: NPC's, even with wands (which aren't that common)
aren't a threat at all in Minetown and above. Just engrave Elbereth
and see your pet take them down. You can even stand in the upstairs
engraving Elbereth, waiting for your pet to take them all down
(they'll come for you), looking afterwards for the downstairs on a
mostly empty level.
4) Peaceful NPC's: See 3

